Here's a comprehensive class template for a stack data structure in C++. This class includes methods for performing operations associated with a stack.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class Stack {
private:
    std::vector<T> elements;     // elements

public:
    // check whether the stack is empty
    bool empty() const {
        return elements.empty();
    }

    // push a new element onto the stack
    void push(const T& item) {
        elements.push_back(item);
    }

    // return the top element of the stack
    T top() const {
        if (elements.empty()) {
            throw std::out_of_range("Stack<>::top(): empty stack");
        }
        return elements.back();      
    }

    // remove the top element of the stack
    void pop() {
        if (elements.empty()) {
            throw std::out_of_range("Stack<>::pop(): empty stack");
        }
        elements.pop_back();
    }

    // return the number of elements in the stack
    size_t size() const {
        return elements.size();
    }
};
```
This Stack class uses the STL vector container to store its elements, providing the following functionalities:

- `empty()` checks whether the stack is empty.
- `push(item)` adds a new element at the top of the stack.
- `top()` returns the top element in the stack.
- `pop()` removes the top element from the stack.
- `size()` returns the number of elements in the stack. 

